{"title":"21/09/2026","markdown":{"headingText":"21/09/2026","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"Recaping to date, I have:\n\n* Set up labbook\n* Set up code repository locally\n* Received code and basic introduction from Dr Turci\n* Partly read introductory papers/books on the topic\n\nLooking forwards, there are 2 more immediate things i need to do:\n\n* continue reading to build up understanding:\n\t* Second quantisation [@secondQuantisationBook2010]\n\t* Introductory paper [@OpenQuantumSystemsIntro2012]\n* Build up main \"computational engine\":\n\t* Look through and understand code Dr Turci has sent me\n\t* Refactor said code into a convenient utility I can use throughout this project\n\nAfter i've done these i'll be equiped with the main theoretical and computational tools for this project. This will inform the rest of the investigation of this project.\n\nHaving a practical understanding of what this project entails should help me understand the theory \"in context\". As such, i think for now its worth examining the code Dr Turci has sent me, in order to start planning the computational engine.\n\n\nExamining the provided julia code (Y4DissertationCodeMaterials/providedCode/basic-1site-bath-julia.ipynb), it starts by reiterating the maths.\nNote; explanation recommends finding a good explanation for \"wick's theorem\". Examining my old personal notes for QFT, i recall it being relevant to evaluating the non commuting terms in the Dyson series. That understanding was based on [@NoNonsenseQFT] which may be of use. However i wonder if the second quantisation [@secondQuantisationBook2010] chapter will introduce it in a more contextually relevant manner.\nSkimming the chapter it seems so...\nChanged my mind, i'm going to try and quickly skim the chapter with the remainder of this afternoon. I expect the concepts will be familiar, so shouldn't take too long. Code examination can come tmr.\n\n### Reading notes: Second quantisation impressions [@secondQuantisationBook2010]:\n\nSecond quantisation seems to be general framework of ladder operators and associated commutation relations; which is a familiar concept. Apparently generalises nicely to N-body quantum physics.\n\nChapter motivates second quantisation by highlighting computational issues w/many body QM due to indistinguishablity, symmetrization.antisymmetrization, and the exchange of particle numbers.\n\nIt then covers the \"apparatus of second quantization\".\n\nCompares representation of labelling particles in state individually to simply counting the number of particles in each state (I.E $\\ket{1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3}$ becomes $\\ket{4, 2, 1}$). This is the \"occuptation number representation\". Also note the \"Fock space\" $\\mathcal{F}$; made up of basis states $\\{ \\ket{n_{i}} \\}$ w/out occupation num condition $\\sum n_i = N$. Therefore many body state can be represented as $\\ket{\\Psi}=\\sum_{n_{i}} c_{n_i}\\ket{n_{i}}$.\n\nWithin this framework, ladder operators can be constructed to act on $\\mathcal{F}$:\n\n$a_{i}^{\\dagger}\\ket{n_{1},...,n_{i},...}=(n_{i}+1)^{1/2}\\zeta^{s_{i}}\\ket{n_{1},...n_{i}+1,...}$\n$s_{i}=\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}n_{j}$\n\nNote that in the fermionic case, the $n_{i}+1$ operation is a mod 2 operation $n_{i}+1 \\equiv (n_{i}+1)\\%2$\nUtility of this; can generate all states by repeated application of $a_{i}^{\\dagger}$ to vacuum state:\n\n$\\ket{n_{1},...}=\\prod_{i}\\frac{1}{(n_{i}!)^{1/2}}(a_{i}^{\\dagger})^{n_{i}\\ket{0}}$\n\n::: {.column-margin}\nWierd notation used by chapter for commutation/anti-commutation relations:\n\n$[A,B]_{\\zeta}=AB-\\zeta AB$\n\n$[A,B]_{\\zeta=+1}=[A,B]_{+}=AB-AB$ // commutator\n\n$[A,B]_{\\zeta=-1}=[A,B]_{-}=AB+AB=\\{A,B\\}$ // anticomutator\n:::\n\nNote that:\n\n$[a_i,a_j^{\\dagger}]_{\\zeta}=\\delta_{ij}$\n\n$[a_i,a_j]_{\\zeta}=0$\n\n$[a_i^{\\dagger},a_j^{\\dagger}]_{\\zeta}=0$","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","css":["../../../styles.css"],"toc":true,"output-file":"log.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-links-title":"Code Links","launch-dev-container-title":"Launch Dev Container","launch-binder-title":"Launch Binder","article-notebook-label":"Article Notebook","notebook-preview-download":"Download Notebook","notebook-preview-download-src":"Download Source","notebook-preview-back":"Back to Article","manuscript-meca-bundle":"MECA Bundle","section-title-abstract":"Abstract","section-title-appendices":"Appendices","section-title-footnotes":"Foot